## Completion evidence (2026-10-15)

The command router now exposes `executeIssue(runId, issueId)` for bounded execution and integration through the existing fake OpenCode HTTP adapter and real Git worktrees. Initial execution and two repairs share one issue ledger, reserved before submission setup. Executor/merger deadline failures, setup failures and failed validation consume that budget. History preserves correlated results, logs, conflict feedback and saved work references. Retry cancels owned activity and reconciles retained branch/HEAD/dirty facts. Exhausted issues remain inspectable and repeated/concurrent calls cannot create an automatic fourth job. Permission denial and cancellation wait without fabricated repairs.

Candidate validation reuses baseline run-local skips across repairs and subsequent validation. Only actual validation timeouts with confirmed tree cleanup add skips. All-skipped validation integrates with `passed_with_omissions`, disclosed with issue attempts in status. New runs retry the original checks; configuration stays byte-for-byte unchanged. Setup timeout, spawn error, setup failure, nonzero exit, denial and cancellation remain distinct; cleanup is never skipped. Live process ownership is persisted during command execution so stop can kill a running check without labelling it timed out. `agentTimeoutMs` is visible in project configuration/run settings/attempt status with a thirty-minute default; tests inject a controllable clock.

TDD red/green evidence: the initial command-boundary repair test failed on the missing router method, then passed with two failed candidate checks and successful integration on attempt 3. Subsequent observed failures exposed setup before reservation, denied validation exhausting the budget, injected clocks not controlling commands, missing retry cancellation/reconciliation, and stop unable to kill a live check and then reporting it as completed. Each was corrected with its behavioral regression green. A final duplicate-failure regression exposed whole-run phase being set failed by an exhausted issue job; issue job settlement now keeps the run active for independent work and final review. Spawn/setup status tests also failed on ambiguous completed/null-exit outcomes before explicit statuses were added. Demonstrations cover success on attempt 3, exhaustion without a fourth dispatch, duplicate results, executor/merger deadlines and late output, skip reuse on repair and future validation, all-skipped qualification, fresh-run checks, setup timeouts and stop cancellation.

Wiki updated: `docs/wiki/features/single-issue-integration.md`; predecessor fixture extracted to `tests/helpers/single-issue.ts` without changing its behavior. No publication, external messages or user-target merge occurred. Native Linux/Windows and credentialed live OpenCode smoke were not run; tests exercise the existing portable command ownership paths on macOS. Automatic dependency scheduling and requirement-question routing remain downstream work.

